@import "./compiler.sly"
@use Build

class ProcessUtils {
    Runs a command, given as the words of its command line, with an optional working directory and an
    optional text handed over stdin, and returns its exit code, its stdout and its stderr once it exits.
    A command that cannot be spawned returns exit code 127 and the reason as its stderr.
}

class SlytherRuntime {
    How a deterministic step of a given lang is run, checked and installed. It is created with the lang and
    the folder the artifacts are built into, and throws when the lang is not one it knows. The langs are:

    - ts: run with `bun`, checked with `bun build --no-bundle`, dependencies in a package.json installed with
      `bun install`, ignoring node_modules
    - py: run with the python of the `.venv` of the artifacts folder, checked with `py_compile`, dependencies in
      a requirements.txt installed with `pip` into the `.venv`, which is created when missing, ignoring `.venv`
    - sh: run with `sh`, checked with `sh -n`, no dependencies

    Every command it returns has its paths relative to the root of the project, since that is where every
    step runs: a step prints paths relative to the root, so its working directory is the root.

    It gives the extension of a script of the lang, the command that runs a script, the command that checks
    its syntax, the file that declares the dependencies of every step of the lang, as a name and a content
    built from a `name: version` map, and the command that installs them, if the lang has any.
}

class SlytherGenerator {
    What writes the scripts and the markdown a step needs, as an abstract class every backend implements
    with two primitives: `ask`, which sends a prompt and returns a JSON reply that matches a schema, along
    with a session that a later `ask` may resume so a fix continues the same conversation, and `execute`,
    which runs a prompt with the tools to read and edit the project, for the llm steps of an operation.

    On top of them it defines `generate`, which takes a #{SlytherGenerationTask} and returns the files it
    asked for, the dependencies they declare and the session, and `fix`, which resumes the session with the
    reason the files failed and returns them corrected. Both throw when a reply lacks an expected file or
    adds one that was not asked for.
}

class SlytherGenerationTask {
    What a #{SlytherGenerator} is asked to write, contains:

    - instructions: The prose of what to write: the rules of the kind, the operation, the step, its params
      and its lang, and the conventions every script follows
    - context: The files the generator may need to read, each with its path and its content, gathered by
      Slyther beforehand so the build never depends on what a backend chooses to explore
    - expected: The paths of the files the reply must contain, relative to the root of the project
    - dependencies: The `name: version` map of what is already installed for the lang, so the reply
      reuses it instead of adding another version or another package that does the same
}

class ClaudeCLIGenerator {
    A #{SlytherGenerator} backed by the Claude Code CLI, `claude` unless the SLYTHER_CLAUDE environment
    variable names another executable. `ask` runs it in print mode with every tool disabled, the prompt
    on stdin, the JSON output format and the schema as its structured output, resuming the session when
    given one, and returns the structured output and the session id of the reply. `execute` runs it in
    print mode in the given directory with the tools to read, search and edit files and to run commands
    allowed, and returns the text of the reply. Either throws when the CLI does not reply, or replies with
    an error.
}

class SlytherVerifier {
    Checks that the script written for a deterministic step works before it is recorded as built, and
    returns why it does not, or nothing when it does. It is created with the root of the project.

    Every script is checked for syntax with the command of its #{SlytherRuntime}. The scripts of the
    read-only operations, locate, list, signature and uses, are also run, since running them changes
    nothing: list with no args must exit 0, and the others must exit 0 or 1 given the first id list
    prints, or a sample id when it prints none. When signature exits 0, every line it prints must have
    the `key shape` form, and when uses exits 0, every line must have the `kind:id key` form.
}
